  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nIsolated to scattered thunderstorms are attempting to develop\nalong a nominal surface dewpoint gradient and marginal upper\nsupport as of 630pm, stretching from central OH into northwest \nPA. Notable mid-level dry air seen via Satellite and ACARS \nsoundings have made storm up growth more of a slog. However, \n~25kts effective shear with mlCAPE values above 2000 J/kg are \nallowing a few more to become organized, especially as their \ninitial updrafts moisten the near-storm air, and offering \ngreater risks for damaging winds with above 1000 J/kg DCAPE \navailable areawide. \n\nFor the rest of the evening, damaging wind is likely to be the\nprimary risk as storms progress east, especially if stronger\ncold pools develop to aide in storm clustering. Flash flooding\nis not likely due to lower rainfall rates (PWAT is down and\nslowly falling) than prior days' values while forward storm \nmotion of 25 mph should limit rain duration at a given location.\nIf any flooding occurs, it will be in areas with poor drainage\nor previously experiencing flooding that sees a storm that\nbetter maximizes rainfall rates.\n\nBoth threats should lower after 10pm but may not completely end\nas elevated buoyancy and shortwave movement maintains varying\nstorm chances before exiting southeast of the region by early\nSunday morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nSurface and shortwave ridging provide for a brief reprieve from\nthe daily rounds of storms on Sunday. \n\nVery broad troughing will remain in place until at least Wednesday \nbefore a stronger shortwave deepens the trough over the Great Lakes. \nWith prevailing W-NW flow and disturbances passing through the upper \npattern, there will be daily chances for showers and thunderstorms, \nwith the potential for more organized convective complexes as hinted \nto in some of the guidance. Precipitable water values remain above-\nnormal in a climatological sense through this period, and as several \nareas already well saturated, will generally take less\nprecipitation to pose a flood risk. As such, WPC does have \nportions of the upper Ohio in a Slight Risk (level 2 of 4) for \nexcessive rainfall Monday- Wednesday. Think it could be very \nwell be possible a Flood Watch is issued at some point during \nthis time frame but there remains some run to run spatial \ninconsistency, which will be better resolved in the 12-24 hour \ntime scale as the day priors convection may shift where the \nthreat is the next day. \n\nIn addition to the flood threat, severe weather chances show an \nuptick as well, particularly earlier in the week according to the \nlong-range machine-learning convective guidance. This makes sense, \nas this period shows the best juxtaposition of instability, moderate \ndeep-layer shear, and decent low-level flow. Some uncertainty \nremains among the various ensemble members that will be better \nresolved in time.",
